  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v10.5.2 — Redis becomes an opt-in runtime feature, and a Web UI ships alongside the server. CONTAINS BEHAVIOUR CHANGES.\n\n⚠️ NEW TRANSITIVE DEPENDENCY: this package now references Delibera.Redis, so consumers pull Delibera.Redis and StackExchange.Redis. Accepted deliberately rather than hidden with PrivateAssets=\"all\", which would remove AddRedisDebateOrchestrator from the published package — the one thing the reference exists to expose. Both packages ship at the same version.\n\nNew — Redis integration, gated by Delibera:Redis:Enabled and off by default. Previously Delibera.Server referenced only Delibera.Core, so AddRedisDebateOrchestrator was never called and the server always ran LocalDebateOrchestrator — a redis service in compose connected to nothing. It now registers the Redis orchestrator and, optionally, the Redis result cache. This buys round events on a Redis Stream (so any API instance can stream them over SSE), shared debate state and optional caching. It does NOT buy distributed turn execution: DebateWorkerService.ProcessMessageAsync is an explicit no-op and each debate still runs in the process that accepted it. A bad connection string now fails at startup naming the key instead of silently degrading, because a deployment that quietly fell back would look distributed and not be; abortConnect=false still covers transient ordering so compose can start Redis and the server in either order.\n\n⚠️ DEPLOYMENT: docker-compose.yml now binds every published port to 127.0.0.1 instead of 0.0.0.0. The API is unauthenticated and unthrottled and a debate spends real LLM credits. A host reaching this server from another machine on its LAN will stop being able to after this upgrade; bind explicitly if that is intended.\n\nTests: 620 passing (493 Core + 117 Server + 10 gRPC contract), 0 failed, 0 skipped, plus 34 Web UI tests. Clean under -warnaserror. The 10.5.0 package page for Delibera.Core shows a 514-test figure; that number was measured at 10.4.0, and at the 10.5.1 tag the count was 611.",
